WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:20.000
Do you know how many choices you make in a typical day?

00:20.000 --> 00:25.240
Do you know how many choices you make in a typical week?

00:25.240 --> 00:31.000
I recently did a survey with over 2,000 Americans, and the average number of choices that the

00:31.000 --> 00:35.360
typical American reports making is about 70 in a typical day.

00:35.360 --> 00:41.800
There was also recently a study done with CEOs in which they followed CEOs around for

00:41.800 --> 00:42.800
a whole week.

00:42.800 --> 00:47.140
And these scientists simply documented all the various tasks that these CEOs engaged

00:47.140 --> 00:52.360
in and how much time they spent engaging in making decisions related to these tasks.

00:52.360 --> 00:58.040
And they found that the average CEO engaged in about 139 tasks in a week.

00:58.040 --> 01:01.680
Each task was made up of many, many, many, many sub-choices, of course.

01:01.680 --> 01:07.660
50% of their decisions were made in nine minutes or less.

01:07.660 --> 01:14.360
Only about 12% of the decisions did they make in an hour or more of their time.

01:14.360 --> 01:16.120
Think about your own choices.

01:16.120 --> 01:19.980
Do you know how many choices make it into your nine minute category versus your one

01:19.980 --> 01:22.060
hour category?

01:22.060 --> 01:27.060
How well do you think you're doing at managing those choices?

01:27.060 --> 01:31.820
Today I want to talk about one of the biggest modern day choosing problems that we have,

01:31.820 --> 01:33.460
which is the choice overload problem.

01:33.460 --> 01:38.460
I want to talk about the problem and some potential solutions.

01:38.460 --> 01:42.160
Now as I talk about this problem, I'm going to have some questions for you, and I'm going

01:42.160 --> 01:44.860
to want to know your answers.

01:44.860 --> 01:50.860
So when I ask you a question, since I'm blind, only raise your hands if you want to burn

01:50.860 --> 01:54.980
off some calories.

01:54.980 --> 02:00.140
Otherwise when I ask you a question, and if your answer is yes, I'd like you to clap your

02:00.140 --> 02:01.180
hands.

02:01.180 --> 02:05.820
So for my first question for you today, are you guys ready to hear about the choice overload

02:05.820 --> 02:09.740
problem?

02:09.740 --> 02:11.120
Thank you.

02:11.120 --> 02:15.480
So when I was a graduate student at Stanford University, I used to go to this very, very

02:15.480 --> 02:18.940
upscale grocery store, at least at that time it was truly upscale.

02:19.020 --> 02:20.900
It was a store called Draeger's.

02:20.900 --> 02:24.900
Now this store, it was almost like going to an amusement park.

02:24.900 --> 02:31.100
They had 250 different kinds of mustards and vinegars and over 500 different kinds of fruits

02:31.100 --> 02:35.180
and vegetables and more than two dozen different kinds of bottled water.

02:35.180 --> 02:38.940
And this is during a time when we actually used to drink tap water.

02:38.940 --> 02:44.740
I used to love going to this store, but on one occasion, I asked myself, well, how come

02:44.740 --> 02:45.740
you never buy anything?

02:45.740 --> 02:47.780
Oh, here's their olive oil aisle.

02:47.820 --> 02:51.900
They had over 75 different kinds of olive oils, including those that were in a locked

02:51.900 --> 02:55.800
case that came from a thousand year old olive trees.

02:55.800 --> 02:59.860
So I one day decided to pay a visit to the manager and I asked the manager, you know,

02:59.860 --> 03:02.900
is this model of offering people all this choice really working?

03:02.900 --> 03:07.500
And he pointed to the busloads of tourists that would show up every day with cameras

03:07.500 --> 03:08.500
ready usually.

03:08.500 --> 03:13.660
We decided to do a little experiment and we picked jam for our experiment.

03:13.660 --> 03:14.900
Here's their jam aisle.

03:14.900 --> 03:17.720
They had 348 different kinds of jam.

03:17.720 --> 03:21.560
We set up a little tasting booth right near the entrance of the store.

03:21.560 --> 03:26.480
We either put out six different flavors of jam or 24 different flavors of jam.

03:26.480 --> 03:28.960
And we looked at two things.

03:28.960 --> 03:34.420
First, in which case were people more likely to, you know, stop, sample some jam?

03:34.420 --> 03:41.280
More people stopped when they were 24, about 60%, than when there were six, about 40%.

03:41.280 --> 03:46.400
The next thing we looked at is in which case were people more likely to buy a jar of jam?

03:46.920 --> 03:48.880
Now we see the opposite effect.

03:48.880 --> 03:54.280
Of the people who stopped when they were 24, only 3% of them actually bought a jar of jam.

03:54.280 --> 03:59.620
Of the people who stopped when they were six, well now we saw that 30% of them actually

03:59.620 --> 04:01.700
bought a jar of jam.

04:01.700 --> 04:05.840
Now if you do the math, people were at least six times more likely to buy a jar of jam

04:05.840 --> 04:10.460
if they encountered six than if they encountered 24.

04:10.460 --> 04:14.400
Now choosing not to buy a jar of jam is actually probably good for us, at least it's good for

04:14.400 --> 04:15.600
our waistlines.

04:15.600 --> 04:21.080
But it turns out that this choice overload problem affects us even in very consequential

04:21.080 --> 04:22.080
decisions.

04:22.080 --> 04:26.960
We choose not to choose even when it goes against our best self-interests.

04:26.960 --> 04:30.400
So now for the topic of today, financial savings.

04:30.400 --> 04:34.600
This is a study that I did with, now I'm going to describe to you a study I did with Gur

04:34.600 --> 04:41.400
Huberman, Amir Kaminika, Wei Zhang, where we looked at the retirement savings decisions

04:41.440 --> 04:48.440
of nearly a million Americans from about 650 plans, all in the US.

04:49.360 --> 04:54.320
And what we looked at was whether the number of fund offerings available in a retirement

04:54.320 --> 05:01.320
savings plan, the 401k plan, does that affect people's likelihood to save more for tomorrow.

05:01.680 --> 05:05.780
And what we found was that indeed there was a correlation.

05:05.780 --> 05:11.260
So in these plans, we had about 657 plans that ranged from offering people anywhere

05:11.260 --> 05:14.580
from two to 59 different fund offerings.

05:14.580 --> 05:20.340
And what we found was that the more funds offered, indeed there was less participation

05:20.340 --> 05:21.340
rates.

05:21.340 --> 05:25.900
So if you look at the extremes, those plans that offered you two funds, participation

05:25.900 --> 05:30.420
rates were around in the mid 70s, still not as high as we want it to be.

05:30.420 --> 05:35.820
In those plans that offered nearly 60 funds, participation rates have now dropped to about

05:35.820 --> 05:38.740
60 percentile.

05:38.740 --> 05:44.780
Now it turns out that even if you do choose to participate, when there are more choices

05:44.780 --> 05:49.660
present, even then it has negative consequences.

05:49.660 --> 05:54.540
So for those people who did choose to participate, the more choices available, the more likely

05:54.540 --> 05:59.180
people were to completely avoid stocks or equity funds.

05:59.180 --> 06:03.340
The more choices available, the more likely they were to put all their money in pure money

06:03.340 --> 06:04.740
market accounts.

06:04.740 --> 06:09.620
Now neither of these extreme decisions are the kinds of decisions that any of us would

06:09.620 --> 06:14.580
recommend for people when you're considering their future financial well-being.

06:14.580 --> 06:19.620
Well over the past decade, we have observed three main negative consequences to offering

06:19.620 --> 06:22.600
people more and more choices.

06:22.600 --> 06:27.460
They're more likely to delay choosing, procrastinate, even when it goes against their best self-interest.

06:27.460 --> 06:31.700
They're more likely to make worse choices, worse financial choices, medical choices.

06:31.700 --> 06:36.100
They're more likely to choose things that make them less satisfied, even when they do

06:36.100 --> 06:37.980
objectively better.

06:37.980 --> 06:43.740
The main reason for this is because, well, we might enjoy gazing at those giant walls

06:43.740 --> 06:48.420
of mayonnaises, mustards, vinegars, jams, but we can't actually do the math of comparing

06:48.420 --> 06:53.240
and contrasting and actually picking from that stunning display.

06:53.240 --> 06:59.080
So what I want to propose to you today are four simple techniques, techniques that we

06:59.080 --> 07:04.720
have tested in one way or another in different research venues, that you can easily apply

07:04.720 --> 07:06.780
in your businesses.

07:06.780 --> 07:09.760
The first, cut.

07:09.760 --> 07:15.440
You've heard it said before, but it's never been more true than today that less is more.

07:15.440 --> 07:18.160
People are always upset when I say cut.

07:18.160 --> 07:20.400
They're always worried they're going to lose shelf space.

07:20.400 --> 07:25.000
And in fact, what we're seeing more and more is that if you are willing to cut, get rid

07:25.000 --> 07:29.840
of those extraneous redundant options, well, there's an increase in sales.

07:29.840 --> 07:31.480
There's a lowering of costs.

07:31.480 --> 07:35.400
There's an improvement of the choosing experience.

07:35.400 --> 07:39.680
When Procter and Gamble went from 26 different kinds of head and shoulders to 15, they saw

07:39.680 --> 07:42.200
an increase in sales by 10%.

07:42.200 --> 07:46.480
When the Golden Cat Corporation got rid of their 10 worst selling cat litter products,

07:46.480 --> 07:49.800
they saw an increase in profits by 87%.

07:49.800 --> 07:54.160
A function of both increase in sales and lowering of costs.

07:54.160 --> 07:57.800
You know, the average grocery store today offers you 45,000 products.

07:57.800 --> 08:01.360
The typical Walmart today offers you 100,000 products.

08:01.360 --> 08:08.920
But the ninth, the ninth largest retailer, the ninth biggest retailer in the world today

08:08.920 --> 08:12.820
is Aldi's, and it offers you only 1,400 products.

08:12.820 --> 08:16.280
One kind of canned tomato sauce.

08:16.280 --> 08:20.040
Now in the financial savings world, I think one of the best examples that has recently

08:20.040 --> 08:24.760
come out on how to best manage the choice offerings has actually been something that

08:24.760 --> 08:29.800
David Laibson was heavily involved in designing, which is the program that they have at Harvard.

08:29.800 --> 08:34.320
Every single Harvard employee is now automatically enrolled in the lifecycle fund.

08:34.320 --> 08:41.120
For those people who actually want to choose, they're given 20 funds, not 300 or more funds.

08:41.120 --> 08:45.400
You know, often people say, I don't know how to cut.

08:45.400 --> 08:46.640
They're all important choices.

08:46.640 --> 08:51.500
And the first thing I do is I ask the employees, tell me how these choices are different from

08:51.500 --> 08:52.500
one another.

08:52.500 --> 08:57.000
And if your employees can't tell them apart, neither can your consumers.

08:57.000 --> 09:02.020
Now before we started our session this afternoon, I had a chat with Gary.

09:02.020 --> 09:08.080
And Gary said that he would be willing to offer people in this audience an all expenses

09:08.080 --> 09:12.640
paid free vacation to the most beautiful road in the world.

09:13.640 --> 09:16.640
Here's a description of the road.

09:16.640 --> 09:20.640
And I'd like you to read it, and now I'll give you a few seconds to read it.

09:20.640 --> 09:26.640
And then I want you to clap your hands if you're ready to take Gary up on his offer.

09:26.640 --> 09:32.640
Anybody who's ready to take him up on his offer, is that all?

09:32.640 --> 09:37.640
All right, let me show you some more about this.

09:38.640 --> 09:41.640
You guys knew there was a trick, didn't you?

09:47.640 --> 09:50.640
Now who's ready to go on this trip?

09:53.640 --> 09:57.640
I think I might have actually heard more hands.

09:57.640 --> 09:58.640
All right.

09:58.640 --> 10:04.640
Now, in fact, you had objectively more information the first time around than the second time

10:04.640 --> 10:08.640
around, but I would venture to guess that you felt that it was more real the second

10:08.640 --> 10:14.640
time around, because the pictures made it feel more real to you.

10:14.640 --> 10:18.640
Which brings me to the second technique for handling the choice overload problem, which

10:18.640 --> 10:20.640
is concretization.

10:20.640 --> 10:25.640
That in order for people to understand the differences between the choices, they have

10:25.640 --> 10:30.640
to be able to understand the consequences associated with each choice, and that the

10:30.640 --> 10:35.640
consequences need to be felt in a vivid sort of way, in a very concrete way.

10:35.640 --> 10:41.640
Why do people spend an average of 15 to 30 percent more when they use an ATM card or

10:41.640 --> 10:43.640
a credit card as opposed to cash?

10:43.640 --> 10:46.640
Because it doesn't feel like real money.

10:46.640 --> 10:51.640
And it turns out that making it feel more concrete can actually be a very positive tool

10:51.640 --> 10:53.640
to use in getting people to save more.

10:53.640 --> 10:59.640
So a study that I did with Shlomi Banarzi and Alessandro Privitero, we looked at, we

10:59.640 --> 11:05.640
did a study with people at ING, employees that are all working at ING, and now these

11:05.640 --> 11:10.640
people were all in a session where they're doing enrollment for their 401K plan.

11:10.640 --> 11:14.640
And during that session, we kept the session exactly the way it used to be, but we added

11:14.640 --> 11:17.640
one little thing.

11:17.640 --> 11:22.640
The one little thing we added was we asked people to just think about all the positive

11:22.640 --> 11:27.640
things that would happen in your life if you saved more.

11:27.640 --> 11:32.640
By doing that simple thing, there was an increase in enrollment by 20 percent, and

11:32.640 --> 11:36.640
there was an increase in the amount of people that were willing to save in terms of the

11:36.640 --> 11:41.640
amount that they were willing to put down into their savings account by 4 percent.

11:41.640 --> 11:43.640
The third technique, categorization.

11:43.640 --> 11:48.640
You know, we can handle more categories than we can handle choices.

11:48.640 --> 11:52.640
So, for example, here's a study we did in a magazine aisle.

11:52.640 --> 11:56.640
Turns out that in Wegeman's grocery stores up and down the northeast corridor, the magazine

11:56.640 --> 12:03.640
aisles range anywhere from 331 different kinds of magazines all the way up to 664.

12:03.640 --> 12:05.640
But you know what?

12:05.640 --> 12:12.640
If I show you 600 magazines and I divide them up into 10 categories versus I show you 400

12:12.640 --> 12:18.640
magazines and divide them up into 20 categories, you believe that I have given you more choice

12:18.640 --> 12:23.640
and a better choosing experience if I gave you the 400 than if I gave you the 600.

12:23.640 --> 12:26.640
Because the categories tell me how to tell them apart.

12:28.640 --> 12:31.640
Here are two different jewelry displays.

12:31.640 --> 12:34.640
One is called Jazz and the other one is called Swing.

12:34.640 --> 12:41.640
If you think the display on the left is Swing and the display on the right is Jazz, clap your hands.

12:44.640 --> 12:45.640
Okay, there's some.

12:45.640 --> 12:50.640
If you think the one on the left is Jazz and the one on the right is Swing, clap your hands.

12:50.640 --> 12:52.640
Okay, a bit more.

12:52.640 --> 12:54.640
Now it turns out you're right.

12:54.640 --> 12:57.640
The one on the left is Jazz and the one on the right is Swing, but you know what?

12:57.640 --> 13:01.640
This is a highly useless categorization scheme.

13:03.640 --> 13:09.640
You know, the categories need to say something to the chooser, not the choice maker.

13:09.640 --> 13:14.640
And you often see that problem when it comes down to those long lists of all these funds.

13:14.640 --> 13:17.640
Who are they actually supposed to be informing?

13:18.640 --> 13:21.640
My fourth technique, condition for complexity.

13:21.640 --> 13:25.640
It turns out we can actually handle a lot more information than we think we can.

13:25.640 --> 13:27.640
We just gotta take it a little easier.

13:27.640 --> 13:30.640
We gotta gradually increase the complexity.

13:30.640 --> 13:33.640
I'm gonna show you one example of what I'm talking about.

13:33.640 --> 13:37.640
Let's take a very, very complicated decision buying a car.

13:37.640 --> 13:42.640
Here's a German car manufacturer that gives you the opportunity to completely custom make your car.

13:42.640 --> 13:46.640
You're gonna make 60 different decisions, completely make up your car.

13:46.640 --> 13:50.640
Now these decisions vary in the number of choices that they offer per decision.

13:50.640 --> 13:54.640
Car colors, exterior car colors, I've got 56 choices.

13:54.640 --> 13:57.640
Engine, gear shift, four choices.

13:57.640 --> 14:03.640
So now what I'm going to do is I'm going to vary the order in which these decisions appear.

14:03.640 --> 14:09.640
So half of the customers are gonna go from high choice, 56 car colors, to low choice, four gear shifts.

14:09.640 --> 14:17.640
The other half of the customers are gonna go from low choice, four gear shifts, to 56 car colors, high choice.

14:17.640 --> 14:19.640
What am I going to look at?

14:19.640 --> 14:22.640
It's not how engaged you are.

14:22.640 --> 14:26.640
If you keep hitting the default button per decision, that means you're getting overwhelmed.

14:26.640 --> 14:29.640
That means I'm losing you.

14:29.640 --> 14:35.640
What you find is the people that go from high choice to low choice, they're hitting that default button over and over and over again.

14:35.640 --> 14:37.640
We're losing them.

14:37.640 --> 14:41.640
They go from low choice to high choice, they're hanging in there.

14:41.640 --> 14:43.640
It's the same information.

14:43.640 --> 14:45.640
It's the same number of choices.

14:45.640 --> 14:50.640
The only thing that I have done is I have varied the order in which that information is presented.

14:50.640 --> 14:54.640
If I start you off easy, I learn how to choose.

14:54.640 --> 15:03.640
Even though choosing gear shift doesn't tell me anything about my preferences for interior decor, it still prepares me for how to choose.

15:03.640 --> 15:10.640
It also gets me excited about this big product that I'm putting together, so I'm more willing to be motivated to be engaged.

15:10.640 --> 15:12.640
So let me recap.

15:12.640 --> 15:18.640
I have talked about four techniques for mitigating the problem of choice overload.

15:18.640 --> 15:21.640
Cut, get rid of the extraneous alternatives.

15:21.640 --> 15:24.640
Conquer ties, make it real.

15:24.640 --> 15:28.640
Categorize, we can handle more categories, less choices.

15:28.640 --> 15:31.640
Condition for complexity.

15:31.640 --> 15:38.640
All of these techniques that I'm describing to you today are designed to help you manage your choices better for you.

15:38.640 --> 15:40.640
You can use them on yourself.

15:40.640 --> 15:42.640
Better for the people that you are serving.

15:42.640 --> 15:49.640
Because I believe that the key to getting the most from choice is to be choosy about choosing.

15:49.640 --> 15:55.640
And the more we're able to be choosy about choosing, the better we will be able to practice the art of choosing.

15:55.640 --> 15:57.640
Thank you very much.

